; -*- mode: lisp -*-
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; What every cable_route variant shares, stated once.
;
; Fourteen documents stand in one cell, on one robot, under one coupled solver
; arrangement, with one tool registrar -- and each of them used to say so.
; This is the column that is true fourteen times; a variant beside it inherits
; every clause here and states only what it changes: its cameras, its step
; budget, its episodes, its routine and its graphs.
;
; What is NOT here, and why. The pegs and the rod are named by every variant
; (`:fixtures` / `:objects`) but placed differently by each -- thirteen layouts
; of spools and cleats, one row of posts, six rod lengths, two terminated runs
; and one anchor grasp -- so they are not shared scenery and this file carries
; no `:fixtures`. Where each stands is `assets/layout.py`'s, which mints one
; placed record per name a document uses (`weave3_peg0`, `wrap_cable`); the rigs
; aimed at those layouts and the samplers that redraw them are `staging.py`'s.
;
; The EPISODE clause is here as the default and overridden by twelve of the
; fourteen. `(:count 1) (:sampler fixed)` is what `wrap` wants -- its start
; state is a robot posture and there is nothing to draw -- and every weave says
; what it varies instead.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(define (suite cable_route)

  (:language move and wind a rod cable with the i2rt YAM under the coupled-proxy MuJoCo+VBD arrangement)

  ; The order is content: the first entry is what a bare `cable_route` resolves
  ; to. weave3 leads because it is the member with a shipped graph; a default
  ; that cannot be demonstrated is a poor front door.
  ;
  ; Then the ladder in order -- weave4, weave5, weave6, whose station count and
  ; stagger depth rise together -- then the three arrangements that sit beside a
  ; rung and change how that level's turning is made, then the two terminated
  ; runs and the industrial panel, then the four drawn boards whose stations are
  ; sampled per episode rather than written down. The reference's wrap keeps its
  ; place at the end. That is the order `difficulty.py` reads them in and the
  ; order a reader of this suite wants.
  (:variants weave3 weave4 weave5 weave6 weave_arc weave_stagger
             port2 port3 port_easy port_medium panel_l2
             board_l1 board_l2 board_l3 board_l4
             wrap)

  (:staging
    (:scene bench_075)
    ; `right` works and `left` holds. Stated on the pairing rather than left to
    ; the embodiment's declaration order, which is what the anchor arm and the
    ; weave's support hand are both read off.
    ;
    ; The arms are solid to each other. Both hands work the same rod -- `wrap`
    ; manipulates with both at once, and a weave hands the cable across the
    ; stations -- so a plan that streams one forearm through the other is a
    ; plan that only worked because the model was not looking. Each arm stays
    ; internally filtered; see EmbodimentSpec.cross_arm_collisions.
    (:embodiment yam_i2rt (:work-arm right) (:support-arm left) (:arms-solid true))
    ; A named arrangement, never a solver config: MuJoCo owns the arms, rigid
    ; VBD owns the rod, and the hands mirror into the VBD side as mass-scaled
    ; proxies. Registered as data in staging.py, with the measurements that set
    ; every number in it.
    (:physics cable_coupled_960hz@v1)
    (:control-mode joint_position)
    (:episodes (:count 1) (:seed 0) (:sampler fixed))
    ; Two arms: the routine lays out an action vector per arm, and `wrap`
    ; genuinely manipulates with both. A one-armed robot is refused here
    ; rather than discovered mid-rollout.
    (:arms 2)
  )

  ; The tools every graph in this suite reaches for -- the latch, the settle,
  ; the route planner, the perception oracle, and the unprivileged half a vision
  ; graph runs on. One registrar serves the wrap and the thirteen weaves, and
  ; each entry carries its own tag: `sim.` is the privilege boundary and the
  ; tags say so per tool rather than wholesale.
  ;
  ; The repro hooks: the latch, standalone, so a repro bundle can replay a
  ; grasp. The solvers need no such twin -- the composer's manifest rebuilds
  ; them.
  (:artifacts
    (:tools "tools:register_cable_tools")
    (:repro-hooks "staging:CABLE_REPRO_HOOKS")
  )

  (:provenance
    (:tier reproduction)
    (:source "newton_tasks/sim/yam_cable_sim.py at defaults: 28-capsule add_rod cable, SolverCoupledProxy mjc+vbd, the reference's keyframe scripts")
  )
)
